#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096423 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "75fd35db-6b4e-5820-b6ac-925d24a0f117" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Left or Right-Opening Parabola with Vertex at the Origin Given a Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "7f3646c9-89b6-58de-a363-cb87e034d9f0" :question/math-academy-id "e-5333" :question/problem "What is the equation of the parabola shown above?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1124/Source/Images/d05aa50677877b505dd8f37cac612630.png)" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that the arms of the parabola open to the left. The parabola has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(-8,4)$.\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n$y^2 = 4px$.\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(-8,4)$, we can substitute $x = -8$ and $y = 4$ into the standard equation and solve for $p$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{2} &= 4px \\\\\n4^{2} &= 4p(-8) \\\\\n16 &= -32p \\\\\np &= -\\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{2} &= 4px \\\\\ny^{2} &= 4(-\\frac{1}{2})x \\\\\ny^{2} &= -2x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the left since\n\n$$\np = -\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nis negative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096424 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17099881-d7b9-54d9-a29a-4f5f28561cd3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Left or Right-Opening Parabola with Vertex at the Origin Given Another Point"}] :question/id #uuid "d66f0111-fe31-5381-ab6d-cd67ee403d28" :question/math-academy-id "e-5334" :question/problem "Find the equation of the right-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(4,4)$." :question/worked-solution "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n$y^2 = 4px$.\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(4,4)$, we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=4$ into the standard equation and solve for $p$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{2} &= 4px \\\\\n4^{2} &= 4p(4) \\\\\n16 &= 16p \\\\\np &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{2} &= 4px \\\\\ny^{2} &= 4(1)x \\\\\ny^{2} &= 4x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096425 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc86c375-c2c4-56b7-a207-0abfc9709f6a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Left or Right-Opening Parabola Given a Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "5546750b-01ad-5aaa-93de-b223297ffc0a" :question/math-academy-id "e-5335" :question/problem "What is the equation of the parabola shown above?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1124/Source/Images/ba4f53d4bcb8d7c3ce8e264ba354d250.png)" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the left. The parabola has its vertex at $(4,2)$ and passes through the point $(0,6)$.\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h, k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(y - k)^{2} &= 4p(x - h)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at\n\n$$\n(h, k) = (4,2)\n$$\n\nwe substitute $h=4$ and $k=2$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(y - 2)^{2} &= 4p(x - 4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(0,6)$, so we can substitute $x=0$ and $y=6$ into the equation above and solve for $p$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(y - 2)^{2} &= 4p(x - 4) \\\\\n(6 - 2)^{2} &= 4p(0 - 4) \\\\\n16 &= -16p \\\\\np &= -1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(y - 2)^{2} &= 4p(x - 4) \\\\\n(y - 2)^{2} &= 4(-1)(x - 4) \\\\\n(y - 2)^{2} &= -4(x - 4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the left since $p = -1$ is negative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096426 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "783ca59b-0242-584c-ae47-004a0eb03817" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Left or Right-Opening Parabola Given the Vertex and Another Point"}] :question/id #uuid "9a27e39b-4a17-578f-830b-3e143c65fd55" :question/math-academy-id "e-5336" :question/problem "Find the equation of the left-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(2,-2)$ and passes through the point $(-6,2)$." :question/worked-solution "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h, k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(y - k)^{2} &= 4p(x - h)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at\n\n$$\n(h, k) = (2,-2)\n$$\n\nwe substitute $h=2$ and $k=-2$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(y + 2)^{2} &= 4p(x - 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(-6, 2)$, so we can substitute $x=-6$ and $y=2$ into the equation above and solve for $p$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(y + 2)^{2} &= 4p(x - 2) \\\\\n(2 + 2)^{2} &= 4p(-6 - 2) \\\\\n16 &= -32p \\\\\np &= -\\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(y + 2)^{2} &= 4p(x - 2) \\\\\n(y + 2)^{2} &= 4(-\\frac{1}{2})(x - 2) \\\\\n(y + 2)^{2} &= -2(x - 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266659 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "75fd35db-6b4e-5820-b6ac-925d24a0f117" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Left or Right-Opening Parabola with Vertex at the Origin Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67bbc023-ac58-5171-908d-2badfbe1d3a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266661} {:answer/id #uuid "63d16d93-ebd9-50d6-8243-2b7d9b616fa3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266662} {:answer/id #uuid "ff3afbb8-119f-511b-a599-963062379f97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266663} {:answer/id #uuid "e257f06d-b2af-57e0-974c-daae06b1d9f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-\\frac{1}{4}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266664} {:answer/id #uuid "b0cefe60-f116-5701-8d83-433ca3e0b8f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266665}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0cefe60-f116-5701-8d83-433ca3e0b8f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266665} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0ccf9bd-7fb1-568b-ad99-4cfc3bfd80ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266660}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1dea1f83-fad7-5576-8d47-906556eb6e45" :question/math-academy-id "q-115625" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13954664/assets/b14a6063e0b1be751e738060087644cacaa3c3cf1e324ee66bf57232834e618d.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the left. The parabola has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(-1,1).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(-1,1),$ we can substitute $x=-1$ and $y=1$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {1}^{2} & =4p(-1) \\\\ 1 & =-4p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(-\\frac{1}{4})x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the left since $p=-\\frac{1}{4}$ is negative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266666 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17099881-d7b9-54d9-a29a-4f5f28561cd3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Left or Right-Opening Parabola with Vertex at the Origin Given Another Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "738f6646-2b0e-54a7-a66f-3f88a8fb5332" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=\\frac{1}{2}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266668} {:answer/id #uuid "df0c7e8f-120b-51df-9fcb-2fe4352cea5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266669} {:answer/id #uuid "b5186bb9-878d-5662-845c-aec58d799d7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=4y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266670} {:answer/id #uuid "38e7bf11-4c43-55d5-9f7a-637bdbf27ae4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-16x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266671} {:answer/id #uuid "13a8146a-a778-5a19-9a8d-732040bc9426" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266672}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df0c7e8f-120b-51df-9fcb-2fe4352cea5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266669} :answer-field/id #uuid "3cdc193e-5221-50b3-b374-abfd779910bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266667}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "03ff4c40-b67c-529b-8313-05d8daa83019" :question/math-academy-id "q-115631" :question/problem "Find the equation of the right-opening parabola that has its vertex at  $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(8,-4).$" :question/worked-solution "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(8,-4),$ we can substitute $x=8$ and $y=-4$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ (-4)^{2} & =4p(8) \\\\ 16 & =32p \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(\\frac{1}{2})x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266673 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17099881-d7b9-54d9-a29a-4f5f28561cd3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Left or Right-Opening Parabola with Vertex at the Origin Given Another Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4f20d5e-58a8-5139-8dd5-e6df1433c437" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=4y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266675} {:answer/id #uuid "32cc0167-455d-558a-9fbc-a2bfaf637780" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-16x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266676} {:answer/id #uuid "af22acc3-5ff2-59da-8d01-efaca6071df3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-\\frac{1}{16}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266677} {:answer/id #uuid "3064d8da-d1e5-5196-8271-d92910221e96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266678} {:answer/id #uuid "18e279b8-1efe-51bb-b2ea-bfd8540b5761" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-\\frac{1}{4}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266679}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "32cc0167-455d-558a-9fbc-a2bfaf637780" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-16x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266676} :answer-field/id #uuid "35176d27-409c-527e-afcc-93a5edf3f3c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266674}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4e805691-ff2e-51c8-aff0-4b1335456a6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-115634" :question/problem "Find the equation of the left-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(-1,-4).$" :question/worked-solution "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(-1,-4),$ we can substitute $x=-1$ and $y=-4$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ (-4)^{2} & =4p(-1) \\\\ 16 & =-4p \\\\ p & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(-4)x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-16x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266680 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc86c375-c2c4-56b7-a207-0abfc9709f6a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Left or Right-Opening Parabola Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08b73b54-8b69-5a59-896f-d8073bfad50c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+1)^{2}=-\\frac{9}{2}(x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266682} {:answer/id #uuid "f3c221b2-f5fd-5ae3-9900-0df1a3001953" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+1)^{2}=-\\frac{9}{8}(x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266683} {:answer/id #uuid "464d585e-eab0-5ca3-b44a-1124e142c210" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)^{2}=\\frac{9}{8}(y+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266684} {:answer/id #uuid "a834de73-80d7-532c-82a6-bb45b080ba37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-1)^{2}=\\frac{9}{8}(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266685} {:answer/id #uuid "37bf17eb-fa70-5cff-895a-07a5b3ebc5af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+2)^{2}=\\frac{9}{2}(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266686}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08b73b54-8b69-5a59-896f-d8073bfad50c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+1)^{2}=-\\frac{9}{2}(x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266682} :answer-field/id #uuid "56e190e5-5c3d-57f2-84d5-74194fc76dd0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266681}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4a6e5633-dfb8-5833-aa29-5b1d91e7af29" :question/math-academy-id "q-115636" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13954664/assets/5742f3f1161782d06b8a785d28d6c59b1e40aec37bd6b62268d6d41e1c662ee0.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the left. The parabola has its vertex at $(-2,-1)$ and passes through the point $(-4,-4).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(-2,-1),$ we substitute $h=-2$ and $k=-1$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+1)^{2} & =4p(x+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(-4,-4),$ so we can substitute $x=-4$ and $y=-4$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+1)^{2} & =4p(x+2) \\\\ (-4+1)^{2} & =4p(-4+2) \\\\ 9 & =-8p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{9}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+1)^{2} & =4p(x+2) \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =4(-\\frac{9}{8})(x+2) \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =-\\frac{9}{2}(x+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the left since $p=-\\frac{9}{8}$ is negative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266687 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc86c375-c2c4-56b7-a207-0abfc9709f6a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Left or Right-Opening Parabola Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a282ecd-9120-5164-9142-e8fed956f6fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-3)^{2}=-\\frac{3}{8}(x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266689} {:answer/id #uuid "01480733-3dea-5b7a-a998-8e6987364ac0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+3)^{2}=3(x+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266690} {:answer/id #uuid "90da7ecc-92e5-566f-b1ae-aa71cd79d6d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+3)^{2}=\\frac{3}{2}(x+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266691} {:answer/id #uuid "6faf3cff-1208-501c-bd13-4ddc53d72dba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-3)^{2}=-12(y-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266692} {:answer/id #uuid "3a407151-85b6-55e5-94c1-77ac3be02ee1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-3)^{2}=12(x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266693}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a407151-85b6-55e5-94c1-77ac3be02ee1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-3)^{2}=12(x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266693} :answer-field/id #uuid "094bad5d-3056-51a0-b6a9-d51a378c2415" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266688}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a7d690cd-8b12-547d-a0c5-09b9dbca554a" :question/math-academy-id "q-115640" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13954664/assets/c88e54f2a7acec3815a53fa4b0d641dd370ccf708b876f8dd231f05e115ea6fc.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the right. The parabola has its vertex at $(3,3)$ and passes through the point $(6,-3).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(3,3),$ we substitute $h=3$ and $k=3$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-3)^{2} & =4p(x-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(6,-3),$ so we can substitute $x=6$ and $y=-3$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-3)^{2} & =4p(x-3) \\\\ (-3-3)^{2} & =4p(6-3) \\\\ 36 & =12p \\\\ p & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-3)^{2} & =4p(x-3) \\\\ (y-3)^{2} & =4(3)(x-3) \\\\ (y-3)^{2} & =12(x-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the right since $p=3$ is positive."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266694 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc86c375-c2c4-56b7-a207-0abfc9709f6a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Left or Right-Opening Parabola Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1456629a-632f-5ab9-bcaa-2d15e43785b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-2)^{2}=\\frac{1}{12}(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266696} {:answer/id #uuid "cbcedba0-8ff8-5837-8b42-1222a6271b0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+2)^{2}=\\frac{1}{3}(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266697} {:answer/id #uuid "858e2d4d-13e4-5f65-8f2f-6978a41bbf4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+2)^{2}=9(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266698} {:answer/id #uuid "92049e69-d2e7-5684-b6a2-06013facd85d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+2)^{2}=-\\frac{9}{4}(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266699} {:answer/id #uuid "ad19b9f2-055b-586b-9a5d-e77023716b64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}=-\\frac{1}{3}(y+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266700}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cbcedba0-8ff8-5837-8b42-1222a6271b0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+2)^{2}=\\frac{1}{3}(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266697} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4d32c64-ecfc-56c6-b87e-29cad4634755" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266695}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "095475ac-3d42-523d-8b3e-46287aade654" :question/math-academy-id "q-115642" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13954664/assets/8ea1ad85f30978bed681f48e02f4af2f401a1f561390bee09683ead252fc1d7d.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the right. The parabola has its vertex at $(1,-2)$ and passes through the point $(4,-3).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(1,-2),$ we substitute $h=1$ and $k=-2$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+2)^{2} & =4p(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(4,-3),$ so we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=-3$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+2)^{2} & =4p(x-1) \\\\ (-3+2)^{2} & =4p(4-1) \\\\ 1 & =12p \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+2)^{2} & =4p(x-1) \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =4(\\frac{1}{12})(x-1) \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{3}(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the right since $p=\\frac{1}{12}$ is positive."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266701 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "783ca59b-0242-584c-ae47-004a0eb03817" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Left or Right-Opening Parabola Given the Vertex and Another Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "caf35401-476f-50d5-adfa-3642e0e91ead" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-1)^{2}=4(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266703} {:answer/id #uuid "40be7211-690e-5c55-a5a7-4dab4d23d264" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+1)^{2}=4(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266704} {:answer/id #uuid "d6f9e7bd-e468-5b25-9079-86fbab116c49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+1)^{2}=x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266705} {:answer/id #uuid "9a3e846f-32f6-5f16-8f5e-f61feac1aeb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-1)^{2}=-4(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266706} {:answer/id #uuid "ca132e3d-7c71-527d-9a9d-4a9bf4df0edb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)^{2}=-(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266707}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40be7211-690e-5c55-a5a7-4dab4d23d264" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+1)^{2}=4(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266704} :answer-field/id #uuid "55868986-7d8f-58a1-895f-439f50031419" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266702}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "25ecddc6-e3d2-533f-a083-0601d1ae3713" :question/math-academy-id "q-115644" :question/problem "Find the equation of the right-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(1,-1)$ and passes through the point $(2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(1,-1),$ we substitute $h=1$ and $k=-1$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+1)^{2} & =4p(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(2,1),$ so we can substitute $x=2$ and $y=1$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+1)^{2} & =4p(x-1) \\\\ (1+1)^{2} & =4p(2-1) \\\\ 4 & =4p \\\\ p & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+1)^{2} & =4p(x-1) \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =4(1)(x-1) \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =4(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266708 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "783ca59b-0242-584c-ae47-004a0eb03817" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Left or Right-Opening Parabola Given the Vertex and Another Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e3e06d9-c495-5903-aa57-d1a15bd490d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-2)^{2}=-16(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266710} {:answer/id #uuid "f813091e-61cf-58f4-8d20-f4b493c0c370" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}=-16(y+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266711} {:answer/id #uuid "a8479efe-2d2b-593b-9941-8faa00a59ce6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+2)^{2}=-4(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266712} {:answer/id #uuid "28165a81-f782-5c55-be03-9b15e4b91edf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+2)^{2}=\\frac{16}{7}(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266713} {:answer/id #uuid "3214b975-1a80-5f33-ac6c-8cce8c08dc37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-2)^{2}=\\frac{4}{7}(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266714}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e3e06d9-c495-5903-aa57-d1a15bd490d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-2)^{2}=-16(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266710} :answer-field/id #uuid "71adc7bd-0a4e-5816-a972-a31624ec4b98" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266709}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "af5ef3a3-53b1-5b4c-810a-f0ac22316bf1" :question/math-academy-id "q-115651" :question/problem "Find the equation of the left-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(-1,2)$ and passes through the point $(-2,6).$" :question/worked-solution "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(-1,2),$ we substitute $h=-1$ and $k=2$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =4p(x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(-2,6),$ so we can substitute $x=-2$ and $y=6$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =4p(x+1) \\\\ (6-2)^{2} & =4p(-2+1) \\\\ 16 & =-4p \\\\ p & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =4p(x+1) \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =4(-4)(x+1) \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =-16(x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266715 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17099881-d7b9-54d9-a29a-4f5f28561cd3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Left or Right-Opening Parabola with Vertex at the Origin Given Another Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "edb3077d-eeda-5dc3-b79c-f796b335c13e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-8x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266717} {:answer/id #uuid "75105eb6-7716-5a06-9451-922804cb471e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266718} {:answer/id #uuid "c641f368-a0eb-5fa6-a43a-b2f490b47a3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=8x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266719} {:answer/id #uuid "20710e9e-58c5-5498-a5ba-a5e4cafe2e28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266720} {:answer/id #uuid "7787c439-3e4b-538a-8ac5-e345b8994bfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266721}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "edb3077d-eeda-5dc3-b79c-f796b335c13e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-8x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266717} :answer-field/id #uuid "55dfbc75-e828-5b1d-95c8-840d0634b2eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266716}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2663faff-8b8b-5a3f-b808-80a34f1d85cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-11751" :question/problem "Find the equation of the left-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(-8,8).$" :question/worked-solution "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(-8,8),$ we can substitute $x=-8$ and $y=8$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {8}^{2} & =4p(-8) \\\\ 64 & =-32p \\\\ p & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(-2)x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-8x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096914 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "75fd35db-6b4e-5820-b6ac-925d24a0f117" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Left or Right-Opening Parabola with Vertex at the Origin Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a17f756f-01f7-5ddf-87d1-f03c882c5a2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{2} = 2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093891} {:answer/id #uuid "adb5274e-2ea8-5709-b15c-c9a9a1c7a4f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{2} = \\frac{16}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093892} {:answer/id #uuid "c0479b2b-23e7-5f07-8b85-2d6de7b34caa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{2} = \\frac{8}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093893} {:answer/id #uuid "5759e391-3aa3-5df6-a046-03e1cba2eedc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{2} = 4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093894} {:answer/id #uuid "6e2e7fc8-ef7c-5bfa-937c-0e8b8f3c84ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{2} = \\frac{4}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093895}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e2e7fc8-ef7c-5bfa-937c-0e8b8f3c84ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{2} = \\frac{4}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093895} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd630118-3a86-59cf-8190-adee10ce7dee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093890}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e658f76e-d8f1-5fc3-aee3-15b3559d9455" :question/math-academy-id "q-11754" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1124/Source/Images/q-11754.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the right. The parabola has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(12,4).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(12,4),$ we can substitute $x=12$ and $y=4$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {4}^{2} & =4p(12) \\\\ 16 & =48p \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(\\frac{1}{3})x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{4}{3}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the right since $p=\\frac{1}{3}$ is positive."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266722 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "75fd35db-6b4e-5820-b6ac-925d24a0f117" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Left or Right-Opening Parabola with Vertex at the Origin Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "957d01a4-bd8b-5bb5-a4fe-0ea865bc7119" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266724} {:answer/id #uuid "6ab794d0-acb9-590a-b9e6-a7e41f2578fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266725} {:answer/id #uuid "a88f675d-6840-581f-9c5c-03e6fe6c00cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266726} {:answer/id #uuid "4bedc011-7d43-573d-8c67-e31f92bc18fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266727} {:answer/id #uuid "4226fcb3-36d4-5b40-bcf7-9acf6f5a7b91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266728}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4bedc011-7d43-573d-8c67-e31f92bc18fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266727} :answer-field/id #uuid "37620f75-70e3-5e0e-9ebd-5024d4622914" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266723}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "60215e1f-40ad-54ae-b2a1-1986343ee299" :question/math-academy-id "q-11756" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13954664/assets/58c92cc542f027ec882191d2b4631f9fcf3704820b2825a18f30dbdc3f59d07b.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the left. The parabola has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(-4,2).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(-4,2),$ we can substitute $x=-4$ and $y=2$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {2}^{2} & =4p(-4) \\\\ 4 & =-16p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(-\\frac{1}{4})x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the left since $p=-\\frac{1}{4}$ is negative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266729 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17099881-d7b9-54d9-a29a-4f5f28561cd3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Left or Right-Opening Parabola with Vertex at the Origin Given Another Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "033224ba-3b4c-5bed-8ccc-9b34855442cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-\\frac{8}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266731} {:answer/id #uuid "cc1e5fc7-988e-577b-a6d4-07423d4ed819" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=\\frac{4}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266732} {:answer/id #uuid "2749846f-ba01-5229-b433-624512db0aaf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-\\frac{16}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266733} {:answer/id #uuid "aec062f0-2dbd-5eb0-9341-c573aaeca55c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=\\frac{8}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266734} {:answer/id #uuid "cbdef78f-8bc9-5385-8bb2-1d6a7e36e5d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-\\frac{4}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266735}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "033224ba-3b4c-5bed-8ccc-9b34855442cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-\\frac{8}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266731} :answer-field/id #uuid "fef4c39e-ea29-5165-8291-137d4b572f62" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266730}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "07838d7d-bdf0-550d-9b73-045741b48872" :question/math-academy-id "q-11757" :question/problem "Find the equation of the left-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(-6,-4).$" :question/worked-solution "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(-6,-4),$ we can substitute $x=-6$ and $y=-4$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ (-4)^{2} & =4p(-6) \\\\ 16 & =-24p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(-\\frac{2}{3})x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-\\frac{8}{3}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266736 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "75fd35db-6b4e-5820-b6ac-925d24a0f117" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Left or Right-Opening Parabola with Vertex at the Origin Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc5407e0-fb8a-5cfb-95c0-88d83a5655f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266738} {:answer/id #uuid "36e5bfb1-e509-5844-816a-af325a539188" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266739} {:answer/id #uuid "7cda709d-4b28-522b-8263-8d55e6f