{2}$ is negative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096918 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17099881-d7b9-54d9-a29a-4f5f28561cd3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Left or Right-Opening Parabola with Vertex at the Origin Given Another Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8fad799b-1adc-5d11-abf8-bbb30fd7ee61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{2} = 3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093915} {:answer/id #uuid "9b1f0659-83a8-54e6-8822-cb011b67caf5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{2} =-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093916} {:answer/id #uuid "b6bc5f6e-613a-55aa-96de-97ff3ea2778d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{2} =-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093917} {:answer/id #uuid "e532045d-233f-5710-a5af-e0eef3536b9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{2} = 2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093918} {:answer/id #uuid "5a2584f3-7c62-5f8c-abb5-f5e062687845" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{2} =-3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093919}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a2584f3-7c62-5f8c-abb5-f5e062687845" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{2} =-3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093919} :answer-field/id #uuid "caae0426-1014-55ad-97d0-169f79598886" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093914}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4df83744-eab5-5358-84fd-8e7f0336e290" :question/math-academy-id "q-11763" :question/problem "Find the equation of the left-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(0, 0)$ and passes through the point $(-12, 6)$." :question/worked-solution "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(-12,6),$ we can substitute $x=-12$ and $y=6$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {6}^{2} & =4p(-12) \\\\ 36 & =-48p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(-\\frac{3}{4})x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266743 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "75fd35db-6b4e-5820-b6ac-925d24a0f117" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Left or Right-Opening Parabola with Vertex at the Origin Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59a79825-0048-5374-871a-337913530b02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-\\frac{8}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266745} {:answer/id #uuid "37bc927a-5eb2-59a3-ab19-31ab8759ae55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-\\frac{16}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266746} {:answer/id #uuid "545ed32a-9620-53a3-803e-05e95feec346" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=\\frac{16}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266747} {:answer/id #uuid "70033e47-9f97-595f-a286-665e0a675967" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=\\frac{8}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266748} {:answer/id #uuid "2085197b-65ca-5569-836d-47dd79c382f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-\\frac{4}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266749}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "37bc927a-5eb2-59a3-ab19-31ab8759ae55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-\\frac{16}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266746} :answer-field/id #uuid "2897e241-7063-5da3-a9b0-7744ca11dc3a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266744}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cf8c8267-197c-5fa6-a8cf-451234107143" :question/math-academy-id "q-11766" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13954664/assets/6723e1c76d63216151e7c78d67c882ae98f9af950675e37ced726e8ce5aea4d3.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the left. The parabola has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(-12,8).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(-12,8),$ we can substitute $x=-12$ and $y=8$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {8}^{2} & =4p(-12) \\\\ 64 & =-48p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(-\\frac{4}{3})x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-\\frac{16}{3}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the left since $p=-\\frac{4}{3}$ is negative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266750 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "783ca59b-0242-584c-ae47-004a0eb03817" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Left or Right-Opening Parabola Given the Vertex and Another Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "295f8b08-db31-5d74-8c49-7680c33eb4e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-4)^{2}=8(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266752} {:answer/id #uuid "316676c5-b207-5ca0-8eb7-7e127d6a4655" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-4)^{2}=-8(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266753} {:answer/id #uuid "7839009f-cb3b-561b-87c2-05a125ad1ada" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-4)^{2}=-4(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266754} {:answer/id #uuid "1da2cb8f-f928-529d-a370-2dbf505caaa9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-4)^{2}=4(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266755} {:answer/id #uuid "fc491c15-266a-58c7-a2f3-b2b562f21758" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-1)^{2}=-8(x-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266756}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "316676c5-b207-5ca0-8eb7-7e127d6a4655" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-4)^{2}=-8(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266753} :answer-field/id #uuid "9eb26fbb-2bef-540d-bb2c-0db2167556de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266751}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d3122981-1a91-5414-84c6-5eaaa9c218b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-11794" :question/problem "Find the equation of the left-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(1,4)$ and passes through the point $(-1,8).$" :question/worked-solution "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(1,4),$ we substitute $h=1$ and $k=4$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-4)^{2} & =4p(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(-1,8),$ so we can substitute $x=-1$ and $y=8$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-4)^{2} & =4p(x-1) \\\\ (8-4)^{2} & =4p(-1-1) \\\\ 16 & =-8p \\\\ p & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-4)^{2} & =4p(x-1) \\\\ (y-4)^{2} & =4(-2)(x-1) \\\\ (y-4)^{2} & =-8(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266757 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "783ca59b-0242-584c-ae47-004a0eb03817" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Left or Right-Opening Parabola Given the Vertex and Another Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ff4c6d0-5c24-5fcd-9fca-9dfd778178ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+1)^{2}=-(x+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266759} {:answer/id #uuid "2d3da3fb-9648-5131-9c99-e17ca9473916" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+1)^{2}=2(x+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266760} {:answer/id #uuid "97c33400-48bb-59ea-9a6c-72d6af92c00c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+4)^{2}=x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266761} {:answer/id #uuid "1ca4baf0-5ed6-5664-b35c-c3a4cfea5285" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+1)^{2}=x+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266762} {:answer/id #uuid "be118941-1ee9-5e27-823e-cc3faced7134" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+1)^{2}=-2(x+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266763}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ca4baf0-5ed6-5664-b35c-c3a4cfea5285" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+1)^{2}=x+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266762} :answer-field/id #uuid "c959057f-8262-51b9-9ad1-345ef50afa38" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266758}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c3bdf088-2670-5350-9585-84f5c57baf89" :question/math-academy-id "q-11800" :question/problem "Find the equation of the right-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(-4,-1)$ and passes through the point $(5,2).$" :question/worked-solution "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(-4,-1),$ we substitute $h=-4$ and $k=-1$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+1)^{2} & =4p(x+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(5,2),$ so we can substitute $x=5$ and $y=2$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+1)^{2} & =4p(x+4) \\\\ (2+1)^{2} & =4p(5+4) \\\\ 9 & =36p \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+1)^{2} & =4p(x+4) \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =4(\\frac{1}{4})(x+4) \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =x+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266764 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc86c375-c2c4-56b7-a207-0abfc9709f6a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Left or Right-Opening Parabola Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33733aa8-212d-5f32-a63f-91b041cb378b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-2)^{2}=-\\frac{16}{3}(x+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266766} {:answer/id #uuid "9407e25c-2e28-53b6-b11e-b1068072c9a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-2)^{2}=-\\frac{8}{3}(x+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266767} {:answer/id #uuid "04c925b0-049b-57fb-a247-a4722706fe2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+4)^{2}=\\frac{8}{3}(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266768} {:answer/id #uuid "493960ae-7ea2-5e9b-8c58-d86b9b5c73f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-2)^{2}=\\frac{16}{3}(x+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266769} {:answer/id #uuid "53b22762-caa2-59a8-b6de-07319cdb4ee6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-2)^{2}=\\frac{8}{3}(x+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266770}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53b22762-caa2-59a8-b6de-07319cdb4ee6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-2)^{2}=\\frac{8}{3}(x+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266770} :answer-field/id #uuid "51ef251d-e219-59a5-99c2-74aa9433e793" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266765}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7f36f5b9-9ac8-5800-8107-0386a4361e15" :question/math-academy-id "q-11801" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13954664/assets/d4d0ad25fe8409b55ad4021c5e06d0f13579cea804b1a2b78b18b3ba76bd0c0e.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the right. The parabola has its vertex at $(-4,2)$ and passes through the point $(2,6).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(-4,2),$ we substitute $h=-4$ and $k=2$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =4p(x+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(2,6),$ so we can substitute $x=2$ and $y=6$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =4p(x+4) \\\\ (6-2)^{2} & =4p(2+4) \\\\ 16 & =24p \\\\ p & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =4p(x+4) \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =4(\\frac{2}{3})(x+4) \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =\\frac{8}{3}(x+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the right since $p=\\frac{2}{3}$ is positive."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266771 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc86c375-c2c4-56b7-a207-0abfc9709f6a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Left or Right-Opening Parabola Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6be78b89-a2b1-5c49-b541-44622f7e9839" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=3(x+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266773} {:answer/id #uuid "2886ed4e-f111-54a9-a561-4954119104ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-3(x+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266774} {:answer/id #uuid "30773eb0-8e48-5ff6-9988-d2aebb7ef7a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+4)^{2}=3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266775} {:answer/id #uuid "9fac91c2-60ab-5a3e-a44c-9441fd351a77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=2(x+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266776} {:answer/id #uuid "2609fed3-abe9-5a2c-b07f-e531745d05c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-2(x+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266777}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6be78b89-a2b1-5c49-b541-44622f7e9839" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=3(x+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266773} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ea9ebdb-5005-5c15-a63d-c09c01cce912" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266772}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "51fd9c57-bec5-5968-83e1-20a46bdee323" :question/math-academy-id "q-11805" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13954664/assets/a16f3f2ba78b5aef180c4331d1dd99f37340d2c6d842c1596e35445f1dbf34c5.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the right. The parabola has its vertex at $(-4,0)$ and passes through the point $(8,6).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(-4,0),$ we substitute $h=-4$ and $k=0$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4p(x+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(8,6),$ so we can substitute $x=8$ and $y=6$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4p(x+4) \\\\ {6}^{2} & =4p(8+4) \\\\ 36 & =48p \\\\ p & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4p(x+4) \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(\\frac{3}{4})(x+4) \\\\ {y}^{2} & =3(x+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the right since $p=\\frac{3}{4}$ is positive."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266778 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "783ca59b-0242-584c-ae47-004a0eb03817" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Left or Right-Opening Parabola Given the Vertex and Another Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c76cf24a-cb45-502d-bee7-9790cb2a8210" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+3)^{2}=2(x-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266780} {:answer/id #uuid "f47546f0-680d-5f07-af34-971f7cc9e728" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+3)^{2}=-3(x-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266781} {:answer/id #uuid "9f283af9-c769-59e5-89b4-5d0b8e4025e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+3)^{2}=3(x-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266782} {:answer/id #uuid "23a2e03b-cd8c-5503-990d-bf4b534d9dd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+3)^{2}=-2(x-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266783} {:answer/id #uuid "c5a9327a-6de3-5a92-b644-158c8315aa53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-4)^{2}=-3(x+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266784}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f47546f0-680d-5f07-af34-971f7cc9e728" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+3)^{2}=-3(x-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266781} :answer-field/id #uuid "92a37ab3-15d7-534d-8498-4f178dbc8414" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266779}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b87f57ef-1931-500e-98ed-5c572750a3ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-11806" :question/problem "Find the equation of the left-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(4,-3)$ and passes through the point $(1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(4,-3),$ we substitute $h=4$ and $k=-3$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+3)^{2} & =4p(x-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(1,0),$ so we can substitute $x=1$ and $y=0$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+3)^{2} & =4p(x-4) \\\\ (0+3)^{2} & =4p(1-4) \\\\ 9 & =-12p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+3)^{2} & =4p(x-4) \\\\ (y+3)^{2} & =4(-\\frac{3}{4})(x-4) \\\\ (y+3)^{2} & =-3(x-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
